<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Lomax</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as John Avery Lomax, John A. Lomax</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Lomax was an American teacher, musicologist and folklorist who worked to preserve American folk music. Raised in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, he studied at the University of Texas and Harvard, where George Lyman Kittredge urged him to collect ballads in the field; his Cowboy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> and Other Frontier Ballads appeared in 1910 with an introduction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>. From 1933 he made recording trips for the Library of Congress with his son Alan, recording Huddie Ledbetter, Lead Belly, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> State Penitentiary, and from 1934 until his death he was honorary curator of the Archive of American Folk Song. He also directed the gathering of ex-slave narratives for the Federal Writers' Project.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-lomax.jpg" alt="John Lomax" width="330" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruby Terrill Lomax · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John-avery-lomax1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lomax was born in Goodman in Holmes County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, to James Avery Lomax and Susan Frances Cooper. In December 1869 the family travelled by ox cart to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, where his father raised horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and corn near the Bosque River in Bosque County. The boy heard cowboy songs from childhood. At about nine he befriended Nat Blythe, a former slave working as a farmhand, who taught him songs and dance steps while Lomax taught him to read and write; the friendship, Lomax wrote later, perhaps gave his life its bent.</p><p>In 1895, at the age of 28, he entered the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> at Austin to study English literature. When he showed the English professor Morgan Callaway the cowboy songs he had written down as a child, they were dismissed as cheap and unworthy, and he burned them. After graduating in 1897 he worked as the university's registrar until 1903.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Kittredge's suggestion of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> branch of the American Folklore Society, Lomax and Professor Leonidas Payne founded the Texas Folklore Society, which had 92 charter members by April 1910. Lomax lectured on cowboy songs and ballads across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, sometimes singing them himself, and in 1912 he was elected president of the American Folklore Society, serving a second term in 1913.</p><p>In 1931 his wife Bess Brown died and the Dallas bank where he worked failed, leaving him in debt and depressed. His son John Lomax Jr. urged him back onto the lecture circuit, and in June 1932 the Macmillan company accepted his proposal for an anthology of American ballads and folksongs. Finding the Library of Congress's recordings inadequate, he arranged to make field recordings for it with equipment paid for by private grants. In June 1933 he and his son Alan, then eighteen, began touring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> prison farms, recording work songs, ballads and blues from prisoners such as James &quot;Iron Head&quot; Baker and Mose &quot;Clear Rock&quot; Platt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Archive of American Folk Song came to hold songs from 33 states and from parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, more than ten thousand discs gathered under the supervision of John and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-lomax">Alan Lomax</a>. In 1936 Lomax became an adviser on folklore collecting to the Historical Records Survey and the Federal Writers' Project. For the narratives of former slaves he insisted that interviews be recorded verbatim, telling workers they &quot;should not censor any materials collected&quot;. Benjamin A. Botkin, who succeeded him in 1938, edited the narratives as Lay My Burden Down in 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Lomax” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The folklorist <b>John Lomax</b> printed it in 1910 in Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads, and reported that he had learned it from a Black saloon keeper in Texas who remembered it from the Chisholm Trail.</blockquote><a href="/songs/home-on-the-range">Home on the Range</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1936 <b>John Lomax</b> found Ethel Best singing &quot;Come by Here&quot; with a group in Raiford, Florida.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumbaya">Kumbaya</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States <b>John Lomax</b> recorded a related version, &quot;The Cambric Shirt&quot;, from Georgia Ann Griffin in Florida in 1939, and other field recordings followed in Arkansas (1958), New York (1966) and Bristol (1976).</blockquote><a href="/songs/scarborough-fair">Scarborough Fair</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Goodman, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Greenville, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.97,-89.91&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Goodman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2247 966 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Lomax"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2697 1236h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2697 1236h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.97,-89.91&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
